          notes: `## Bible references
**Matthew 3:1–17; 4:1–11; Mark 1:1–13; Luke 3:1–22; 4:1–13**.

## John the Baptist
John preached in the **wilderness of Judea** a **baptism of repentance for the forgiveness of sins**, fulfilling Isaiah: "the voice of one crying in the wilderness: **Prepare the way of the Lord**".
- he called the Pharisees and Sadducees a "**brood of vipers**" and demanded **fruit that befits repentance**; descent from Abraham was no protection — "the **axe is laid to the root** of the trees"
- practical advice (Luke): share coats and food; tax collectors — **collect no more than is appointed**; soldiers — **rob no one** and be content with your wages
- he pointed to one **mightier**, whose sandals he was not worthy to carry, who would baptise with the **Holy Spirit and with fire**

## The baptism of Jesus
Jesus came from Galilee to the Jordan. John tried to prevent Him: "**I need to be baptised by you.**" Jesus replied that it was fitting "**to fulfil all righteousness**". As He came up, the heavens opened, the **Spirit descended like a dove**, and a voice said, "**This is my beloved Son, with whom I am well pleased.**"

### Significance
- Jesus, though sinless, **identified with sinners**
- He endorsed John's ministry
- He was **anointed** with the Spirit and publicly declared **Son of God** — the start of His ministry
- Father, Son and Spirit are revealed together
- an example for Christian baptism

## The temptation
Led by the **Spirit** into the wilderness, Jesus fasted **forty days and forty nights**. Each test began, "**If you are the Son of God**".
1. **Stones to bread** — use power to satisfy His own need (and win people by material things). Reply: "**Man shall not live by bread alone**, but by every word that proceeds from the mouth of God."
2. **Pinnacle of the temple** — throw Himself down; the devil **quoted Scripture** (Psalm 91). A call to win followers by spectacle and to test God. Reply: "**You shall not tempt the Lord your God.**"
3. **A very high mountain** — all the kingdoms of the world in exchange for worship: political power by compromise. Reply: "**Begone, Satan!** … You shall worship the Lord your God and **him only shall you serve**."
All three replies come from **Deuteronomy**. Luke places the temple test last and adds that the devil departed "**until an opportune time**". Matthew and Mark record that **angels ministered** to Him.

## Lessons
temptation itself is not sin; it often follows spiritual high points and comes in moments of weakness; Scripture can be misused; know and use God's word; reject shortcuts to success; put God first.`,
