          notes: `## Bible references
**Mark 1:16–20; 2:13–17; Matthew 8:18–22; Luke 9:57–62; 14:25–33**.

## The first disciples
By the **Sea of Galilee** Jesus saw **Simon and Andrew** casting a net: "**Follow me and I will make you become fishers of men.**" **Immediately** they left their nets. A little further He called **James and John**, sons of **Zebedee**, who were **mending the nets**; they left their father in the boat **with the hired servants**.
*Note:* the response was immediate; they left **occupation and family**.

## The call of Levi
Jesus saw **Levi son of Alphaeus** (Matthew) sitting at the **tax office** and said, "Follow me." He rose and followed. At a meal in his house many **tax collectors and sinners** sat with Jesus. The scribes of the Pharisees objected. Jesus answered: "**Those who are well have no need of a physician**, but those who are sick; **I came not to call the righteous, but sinners**."
*Note:* no one is too sinful to be called; discipleship crosses social barriers.

## Would-be followers
- a **scribe**: "I will follow you wherever you go." — "**Foxes have holes, and birds of the air have nests; but the Son of man has nowhere to lay his head.**" (expect hardship, not comfort)
- another: "Let me first go and **bury my father**." — "**Leave the dead to bury their own dead.**" (the kingdom takes priority over family duty)
- another: "Let me first say **farewell** to those at my home." — "**No one who puts his hand to the plough and looks back** is fit for the kingdom of God." (no divided loyalty)

## The cost (Luke 14)
- love for Christ must exceed love for **father, mother, wife, children** — even one's own life
- "whoever does not **bear his own cross** and come after me cannot be my disciple"
- **count the cost**: the man building a **tower** first sits down to see whether he can finish, or he is mocked; a **king** with ten thousand weighs whether to meet one who comes with twenty thousand, or asks for terms of peace
- "whoever does not **renounce all that he has** cannot be my disciple"

## Demands summarised
prompt obedience; self-denial; readiness to suffer; total commitment; putting Christ above possessions and relationships; perseverance; witnessing ("fishers of men").

## Application
a Christian student resists peer pressure, accepts ridicule for doing right, uses time and gifts for God, and does not turn back when faith becomes costly.`,
