          notes: `## Bible references
**Mark 4:35–41; Matthew 14:13–33; Luke 7:11–17; Mark 5:21–43**.

## Why miracles?
They show Jesus' **compassion**, reveal His **identity and authority**, are signs that the **kingdom of God has come**, and call for **faith**.

## The stilling of the storm
A great storm arose; waves beat into the boat. Jesus was **asleep on the cushion in the stern**. The disciples cried, "Teacher, **do you not care if we perish?**" He rebuked the wind and said to the sea, "**Peace! Be still!**" There was a great calm. "**Why are you afraid? Have you no faith?**" They were filled with awe: "**Who then is this, that even wind and sea obey him?**"
*Meaning:* Jesus is Lord over nature; He was truly human (tired, asleep) yet divine; faith dispels fear.

## Feeding the five thousand
Hearing of John's death, Jesus withdrew to a lonely place; crowds followed, and He had **compassion** and healed their sick. At evening the disciples wanted to send them away. "**You give them something to eat.**" They had only **five loaves and two fish**. He looked up to heaven, **blessed, broke** and gave to the disciples, who gave to the crowds. All ate; **twelve baskets** of pieces were left; about **five thousand men**, besides women and children.

## Walking on the sea
He made the disciples go ahead and went up the mountain **to pray**. In the **fourth watch** He came walking on the sea; they cried, "**It is a ghost!**" — "**Take heart, it is I; have no fear.**" **Peter** walked toward Him, but seeing the wind he was afraid and began to sink: "**Lord, save me.**" Jesus caught him: "**O man of little faith, why did you doubt?**" Those in the boat worshipped: "**Truly you are the Son of God.**"

## The widow's son at Nain
Near the gate of **Nain** a dead man was being carried out — **the only son of his mother, and she was a widow**. The Lord had compassion: "**Do not weep.**" He touched the **bier**: "**Young man, I say to you, arise.**" He sat up and spoke. The people said, "**A great prophet has arisen among us**" and "God has visited his people."
*Note:* nobody asked; the miracle sprang from compassion alone.

## Jairus' daughter
**Jairus**, a **ruler of the synagogue**, begged Jesus to lay hands on his dying daughter. On the way a woman who had suffered a **flow of blood for twelve years** touched His garment and was healed: "**Daughter, your faith has made you well.**" News came that the girl was dead. "**Do not fear, only believe.**" Taking **Peter, James and John**, He said the child was "not dead but sleeping" — they laughed. He took her hand: "**Talitha cumi**" ("Little girl, I say to you, arise"). She walked; she was **twelve years old**. He ordered them to tell no one and to **give her something to eat**.

## Lessons
bring every need to Jesus; little becomes much in His hands; keep your eyes on Christ, not the storm; He is Lord of life and death.`,
