          notes: `## Bible references
**John 2:1–11; 4:46–54; 5:1–18; 9:1–41; 11:1–45; 20:30–31**.

## Signs
John calls the miracles "**signs**" — acts that point beyond themselves to **who Jesus is**.

## Water into wine (Cana)
At a wedding in **Cana of Galilee** the wine ran out. Jesus' mother told the servants, "**Do whatever he tells you.**" There were **six stone jars** for the Jewish rites of purification. Jesus had them filled with water; the **steward of the feast** tasted and told the bridegroom, "You have kept **the good wine until now**." This, the **first of his signs**, **manifested his glory**, and His disciples **believed in him**.

## The official's son
At Cana an **official** from **Capernaum** begged Jesus to come before his son died. "**Go; your son will live.**" The man **believed the word** and went. His servants met him: the fever left at **the seventh hour** — the very hour Jesus spoke. He and his household believed. The **second sign**.

## The pool with five porticoes
In Jerusalem, by the **Sheep Gate**, lay a man ill for **thirty-eight years**. "**Do you want to be healed?**" He had no one to put him into the pool when the water was troubled. "**Rise, take up your pallet, and walk.**" It was the **Sabbath**; the Jews objected to his carrying the pallet. Jesus later told him, "**Sin no more**, that nothing worse befall you." He answered His critics, "**My Father is working still, and I am working**" — they sought to kill Him because He made Himself **equal with God**.

## The man born blind
The disciples asked, "Who sinned, this man or his parents?" — "**It was not that this man sinned, or his parents**, but that the works of God might be made manifest in him." Jesus made **clay with spittle**, anointed his eyes and sent him to wash in the pool of **Siloam** ("Sent"). He came back seeing. The Pharisees questioned him and his **parents**, who feared being put out of the synagogue: "**He is of age; ask him.**" The man said, "**One thing I know, that though I was blind, now I see**"; they **cast him out**. Jesus found him; he said, "**Lord, I believe**," and worshipped. Those who claim to see are the truly blind.

## The raising of Lazarus
**Lazarus of Bethany**, brother of **Mary and Martha**, fell ill. Jesus **stayed two days longer**: the illness was "**for the glory of God**". When He arrived Lazarus had been in the tomb **four days**. Seeing Mary and the Jews weeping, "**Jesus wept**". At the tomb He prayed and cried, "**Lazarus, come out.**" The dead man came out, bound with bandages: "**Unbind him, and let him go.**" Many believed; but the council, led by **Caiaphas**, planned to put Jesus to death.

## The purpose
"These are written **that you may believe that Jesus is the Christ, the Son of God**, and that believing you may **have life in his name**."

## Lessons
obey Christ's word; faith grows from hearing to seeing; suffering is not always the result of personal sin; religious pride blinds; Jesus is Lord of life.`,
